<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="LZWplndZiFzwB8k86UhikM" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="RZtxzotb6R9c9DpROHxkFW" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="ALviiSbrFZ0LqJWalGJv69" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="UiM985uksmqTv8kAc2TLcd" Location="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="RhYTGB8mMBQI8dK5DfIWsq" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="V7G10nnAOXU2VS5yXL9DGA" Location="VL.GeoJSON" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="AP7VsnnxVFRJUltsLCr5Lc">
    <Canvas Id="KLPFE0a8Ao5yK0cUDG0qsg" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="H7cQt5WAS6roaoDGdnx6pS">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="MzixpATKjdoJzm1djT4u7S">
        <Canvas Id="MUQ3se5A93CW3iHL2SOphC" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="BJPXXN0QUQmwNdaSDhmZj9" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1900" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Prompt - Grow a town.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Two orange dots, 700 m apart as the crow flies, each standing on one 60 m stub of street. Press&#xD;&#xA;GROW. Every dead end pushes out one more 60 m of street - straight on, or round a corner - and&#xD;&#xA;now and then a side street branches off. Press it again. And again. FOUND stays False while the&#xD;&#xA;two little towns grow towards each other; on the ninth press they touch, FOUND flips to True and&#xD;&#xA;a teal route appears between the dots: 1080 m. Two presses later it is 960 m. The dots never&#xD;&#xA;moved. A new junction somewhere made a shorter way, and ShortestPath found it without being told.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Then it stops falling, and that is the second lesson. Every street here runs north-south or&#xD;&#xA;east-west, so any route from A to B that never doubles back is exactly 600 + 360 = 960 m - the&#xD;&#xA;'Manhattan distance'. The crow's 700 m is unreachable on a grid, however many streets you add.&#xD;&#xA;The first route was 1080 because it doubled back once (60 m out, 60 m home); the town closed that&#xD;&#xA;detour and hit the floor. A town that could grow diagonals would keep shrinking. This one cannot,&#xD;&#xA;and the number tells you so.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT THE RULE IS. A street is one 60 m segment on a 60 m grid. A dead end is a street end that no&#xD;&#xA;other street end touches. On GROW each dead end grows one child (a third of the time it turns&#xD;&#xA;left, a third right, a third straight on), and when the child went straight, four times in ten a&#xD;&#xA;second child branches off sideways. A child that lands on a grid point where a street already&#xD;&#xA;ends IS joined to it - that is the whole snapping rule, and it is why the grid matters: two&#xD;&#xA;streets that cross between grid points do not meet (BuildNetwork joins ENDPOINTS, not crossings,&#xD;&#xA;exactly as Tutorial 13 said - and on a 60 m grid of 60 m streets every crossing IS an endpoint).&#xD;&#xA;Nothing grows past the edge of the frame, and nothing grows past 200 streets. Around the&#xD;&#xA;seventeenth press every tip has run into something and the town holds at about 167 streets.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The turns are not random. Each grid point hashes its own coordinates into a number between 0 and 1&#xD;&#xA;- the classic frac(sin(x*12.9898 + y*78.233)*43758.5453) that every shader programmer has typed -&#xD;&#xA;so the same tip turns the same way every run. Press RESET and GROW the same number of times: the&#xD;&#xA;same town. Randomness you can reproduce is randomness you can debug - and the numbers above were&#xD;&#xA;predicted by running the same rule in a twenty-line script before this patch was ever opened.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NETWORKS BUILT climbs by one per press, by design: the town really is a new list of streets each&#xD;&#xA;time, and BuildNetwork rebuilds. Between presses it holds still, because the list is the same&#xD;&#xA;object frame after frame. Prompt Which door held it at 1 with a fixed town; this patch is the other&#xD;&#xA;half of the same contract - the counter reports change when there IS change. Watch what it does&#xD;&#xA;once nothing is left to grow.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;SAVE writes the current town to FILE PATH through Write GeoJSON, indented, one Feature per street.&#xD;&#xA;Open it in a text editor: LineStrings with coordinates like [-300, -180]. The file says nothing&#xD;&#xA;about what those numbers mean - RFC 7946 has no field for it, and this town is in metres on a flat&#xD;&#xA;canvas, not degrees on a sphere. Prompt Which door's file admits that with a 'units' property on&#xD;&#xA;every feature. This one does not; that is yours to add. Then point Which door's FILE PATH at it&#xD;&#xA;and see whether its reader accepts a town it did not write.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT THIS IS NOT. A toy, not a planner. Real growth models - space colonisation, tensor fields,&#xD;&#xA;Parish and Mueller's L-system city - grow along fields and constraints and never stop at 200.&#xD;&#xA;Streets here are straight, 60 m, axis-aligned, and join only at ends. The hash is not a random&#xD;&#xA;number generator, and the grid is doing most of the work the design called 'snapping'.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE MACHINERY, top to bottom. Two seed streets -&gt; FrameDelay holds the town from one frame to the&#xD;&#xA;next (its Initial Value is the seed). A Cache region does all the growing and only runs when the&#xD;&#xA;town changes: one loop reads every street's two ends; one nested loop counts, for every tip, how&#xD;&#xA;many ends sit on it (1 = dead end - Keep); two loops make the children (straight-ish, and the&#xD;&#xA;branch); one loop turns the coordinate pairs back into LineStrings; one loop wraps the streets as&#xD;&#xA;Features for Write GeoJSON. Switch (Boolean) decides whether this frame's town is the grown one&#xD;&#xA;(GROW pressed, under 200 streets) or the same one; RESET puts the seed back. BuildNetwork and&#xD;&#xA;ShortestPath read the held town every frame.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS. Network / graph, node (a street end), edge (a street), connectivity (FOUND), dead end&#xD;&#xA;(a degree-1 node), Manhattan distance (the grid's own metric), procedural generation. Snapping:&#xD;&#xA;moving a new point onto an existing one within a tolerance - here the grid IS the tolerance.&#xD;&#xA;Feature: one geometry plus its properties, which this file leaves empty.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION. L-systems, Conway, the Coding Train's space colonisation - anything where a&#xD;&#xA;few local rules produce a shape nobody drew. The GIS half is that the shape is then MEASURED: a&#xD;&#xA;route is a number, and the number falls as the structure gains loops - until the geometry's own&#xD;&#xA;floor stops it. Change the turn thirds, the branch odds, the frame - and watch the number.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT. Prompt Which door reads a town like this from a file - point it at the one you saved.&#xD;&#xA;Tutorial 13 is where BuildNetwork and ShortestPath first appeared, with one bridge to close.&#xD;&#xA;Tutorial 05 grew a shape; this grew a network. What would grow a surface?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Sources: none for the exercise (no desktop GIS has a frame loop to press). The hash is folklore&#xD;&#xA;attributed to no one; Parish and Mueller, 'Procedural Modeling of Cities', SIGGRAPH 2001, is cited&#xD;&#xA;and not implemented.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
